I see the issue — the new Tools band renders two AxisBars with `axis="overall"` (using `DISCRIMINATION.calibBad=1.5` and `DISCRIMINATION.baseline=6.4`), which adds `axis-value-overall` cells to the DOM that the existing test (`getAllByTestId('axis-value-overall')`) doesn't expect. The test loops over all axes and asserts every cell matches `BASELINE_ITERATION.axes[axis]`, but the Tools band adds "overall" cells with 1.50 and 6.40.

I need to update the test to be more specific about which cells come from the baseline card vs the Tools band. Looking at the test:

```js
for (const axis of ENCORE_AXES) {
  const val = BASELINE_ITERATION.axes[axis];
  const cells = screen.getAllByTestId(`axis-value-${axis}`);
  // Both the hero strip and the baseline card carry the axis value.
  expect(cells.length).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(1);
  for (const cell of cells) {
    expect(cell.textContent, `${axis} axis value`).toBe(val.toFixed(2));
  }
}
```

The problem: when `axis === "overall"`, the Tools band adds two more cells (1.50 and 6.40), and 1.50 ≠ 6.40 so the assertion fails.

The cleanest fix: scope the test query to the section (hero or baseline) rather than the whole DOM. But actually, the simplest fix is to limit the query to the two sections that carry the baseline values (hero + baseline card). Looking at the band structure, the hero strip and the baseline card each have their own AxisBars. The Tools band has its own AxisBars with different values.

The best approach: query within specific sections using `within()`. Let me update that test.
